 ## Overview
-StereoGaussians is a feed-forward method that predicts a metric 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representation from a single calibrated stereo pair, enabling nearby-view extrapolation without per-scene optimization [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its siblings by freezing a pretrained stereo encoder to extract geometric anchors and features, while a trainable predictor maps these to Gaussian attributes on an expanded image canvas with a dedicated back layer for disoccluded content [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [AnySplat Pipeline](12847-anysplat-pipeline.md) or [Splatt3R](16423-splatt3r.md) which may use different feature aggregation or multi-view inputs, StereoGaussians relies on a single stereo pair and explicit depth anchoring [S1, unofficial].
+StereoGaussians is a feed-forward method that predicts a metric 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representation from a single calibrated stereo pair, enabling nearby-view extrapolation without per-scene optimization [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by transferring frozen stereo features into metric 3DGS prediction using a two-layer Gaussian structure and an expanded image canvas to support disoccluded and outside-field-of-view content [S1, unofficial]. Unlike methods that synthesize images directly (e.g., LVSM) or refine inverse depth through a composer (e.g., SHARP), StereoGaussians uses explicit feed-forward multi-view Gaussian prediction with fixed front-layer stereo depth and bounded image-plane offsets [S1, unofficial].
 
